A component that calls itself is left as a call on every target. Three
substitutions can replace a component with its body, and each has to decline
this one or there is no finite body to produce: `passInlinePure`,
`passNoInlineComponents`, and `optimize.inlineComponentCall`. The third did
not.

It was reachable only after lowering, and only through the prop lowering adds.
`internal/build` runs the optimizer a second time, after `Lower`, so that what
the passes synthesize gets folded -- and by then `passRecursionDepth` has given
every recursive component a `__depth int = 0` prop with a literal at the call
site. That is a prop that folds, which is the whole of what
`inlineComponentCall` asks before substituting; before it existed, `TreeView`'s
only prop was a value that never folds and the call was left alone.

`maxInlineDepth` is a backstop and not an answer: it stops at 8, by which point
a body with *two* self-calls has been copied 2^8 times. examples/showcase built
one composable of 2340 lines that way and Kotlin refused it outright --
`MethodTooLarge: MainScreenKt.MainScreen`, the JVM's 64KB per-method limit --
after first exhausting the compiler's heap.

The walk that answers it is `ir.ComponentSelfRefs`, shared with the lowering:
the copy there listed `*ir.ContextProvider` among the statements that cannot
host a node, so a self-call under a context override was not a self-call to it.

deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `__depth = 1` -- the body is not copied per level, so no call site carries a depth the optimizer counted to
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `Depth: 1` -- the same on a target whose recursion is a function call

